N,G|               The following text fades in over black:                              
N|               This is a true story. The events depicted took place in              
N|               Minnesota in 2006. At the request of the survivors, the              
N|               names have been changed. Out of respect for the dead, the            
N|               rest has been told exactly as it occurred.                           
O|
T|                                                        FLARE TO WHITE:             
O|
S|               EXT. TWO LANE HIGHWAY. RURAL MINNESOTA - DAY                         
O|
N|               The white becomes snowfall, a blizzard. Through it we can            
N,G|               make out a two lane road. A car emerges from the snow --             
N,G|               rust-spotted, chains on the tires -- coming towards us.              
T|                                                                CUT TO:             
O|
S|               INT. CAR (TRAVELING) - DAY                                           
O|
N,I|               LORNE MALVO, age unknown, birth place unknown, sits behind           
N|               the wheel, his breath white with frost. If he minds he               
N|               doesn’t show it.                                                     
O|
N|               As he drives we become aware of THUMPING coming from inside          
N|               the trunk. As if someone is in there who doesn’t want to be.         
N|               Malvo ignores it.                                                    
N|               A DEER                                                               
O|
N|               appears in the road ahead of him. Malvo turns too late, HITS         
N|               it.  The CAR SKIDS off the road, ROLLS twice -- the trunk            
N|               popping open -- before coming to rest on its wheels.                 
O|
N,I|               Beat. A MAN emerges from the trunk wearing only underwear.           
N|               He is stunned from the crash, but sound enough of mind to            
N|               know this is his chance. As we watch he LOPES off through            
N|               waist-deep snow, making for the tree line.                           
O|
N|               Beat.  The driver’s door opens. Slowly, Malvo climbs out.            
N|               His head is bleeding where it hit the windshield. He stands          
N|               unsteadily in the snow, getting his bearings.                        
G|               ANGLE ON THE OTHER MAN                                               
O|
N|               Loping off through the snow. Malvo could catch him if he             
N|               tried, but he makes no attempt. Instead he walks into the            
N|               center of the road where the deer lays on its side,                  
N|               struggling to get up. Three of its four legs are broken, but         
N|               still it fights for life. Malvo stands over the wounded              
N|               animal, looking down.                                                
O|
O|
O|                                                                      2.            
O|
G|               CLOSE UP ON THE DEER                                                 
N|               It’s eyes wild, blood bubbles foaming from its nose and              
N|               mouth, mortally wounded.                                             
O|
G|               CLOSE UP ON MALVO                                                    
O|
N|               Studying it’s eyes. What does he see in there?                       
O|
N|               We begin to hear a strange throbbing, churning sound, half           
N|               animal, half machine.                                                
O|
T|                                                                CUT TO:             
S|               INT. KITCHEN. NYGAARD HOUSE. SAINT CLOUD, MINNESOTA - DAY            
O|
N,I|               LESTER NYGAARD, 40, home for lunch, sits at the kitchen              
N|               table.  Lester is the kind of guy who apologizes when you            
N,I|               step on his foot. His wife, PEARL, 39, is heating up a can           
N|               of soup at the stove. We get the sense she has been talking          
N|               nonstop since Lester walked in the door.                             
O|
